@* ================= FAQ ================= *@
<section class="section" id="faq">
    <div class="container container-tight" style="max-width:820px;">
        <div class="text-center mb-5">
            <div class="section-kicker mb-2">FAQ</div>
            <h2 class="h2-section mb-0">Straight answers</h2>
        </div>

        <details class="faq-item" open>
            <summary>Is my scraped data resold or used to train anything?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">No. Under the bring-your-own model your browser, your storage, and your vault stay yours — nothing is resold. <code>capture</code> streams full documents straight to <em>your</em> storage, never touching our database, and your payloads are your JSON, exportable any day. The only signal we keep is anonymized selector-drift telemetry (which selector broke, on which host, when) — never your extracted content.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What browsers can I connect?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">Anything that speaks CDP. Crawldad always dials <em>out</em> to your endpoint: Browserbase, Browserless, a self-hosted TLS-terminated CDP edge, or local Chromium exposed through an <code>ngrok</code>/<code>cloudflared</code> tunnel. The <code>IBrowserBackend</code> seam is multi-backend by design, so no single provider is load-bearing — and the browser is always billed to your account, not ours.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How is this different from just writing Playwright?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">A Playwright codebase is a program you own, operate, and watch rot. A Crawldad payload is <em>data</em>: declarative JSON that's versioned, diffable, pinned, replayable, and drift-detected — with a safe expression language that can't <code>eval</code>, loop unboundedly, or reach the filesystem. You author the automation; we own the interpreter, the retry taxonomy, the checkpoint saga, and the drift tooling.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What happens when I hit my concurrent-slot cap?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">We queue, we don't reject. Run N+1 is accepted, persisted <code>queued</code>, and returns its position — a <code>429</code> at your own capacity limit feels like a broken product, while a visible queue feels like a full one. When a slot frees, your oldest queued run is promoted automatically. A hard <code>429</code> only ever comes from exceeding the per-tier queue depth, and sustained wait is the honest "add slots?" signal.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How are credentials handled?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">Always by reference, never by value. A <code>secretRef</code> is a vault reference consumable only by <code>fill.secret</code>, which resolves it at fill time and types it straight into the field — it never enters an expression, a variable, or the trace (which records only <code>secret:&lt;name&gt;</code>). Browser connect secrets are encrypted at rest and never echoed by any endpoint or log.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Why is Crawldad closed-source and hosted-only?<span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg></span></summary>
            <div class="faq-body">Because every automation runs through one hosted plane, cross-customer drift signal accretes — turning "this selector broke on this host" into proactive monitoring nobody running solo could build. Hosted-only keeps that signal intact. Transparency is delivered through the <em>product</em>, not the source: your payloads, your runs, and your data stay yours and exportable at all times.</div>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>

